import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = '/misc/amusement-parks/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Park Days — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Every amusement park day from 2021 to 2024: Pacific Park on the Santa Monica Pier, Halloween Horror Nights at Universal Hollywood, Six Flags Magic Mountain, Universal Orlando and SeaWorld, Disneyland at Halloween and LEGOLAND on Christmas Day.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2021–2024</p>
  <h1>Park Days</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Wheels, coasters, castles and one very convincing Springfield.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>7 park days · October 2021 – December 2024</strong><br />
    2 Universal Studios (Hollywood and Orlando) · 2 Halloweens · 1 Christmas Day · 1 pier · 1 SeaWorld · 1 Six Flags<br />
    Most photographed: Universal Hollywood at night · least photographed: every ride
  </div>

  <p>
    Amusement parks are strange places to photograph. The best parts, the drops and loops and screaming, happen
    when your phone is zipped into a pocket, and what is left in the camera roll is the queue, the castle, the
    snacks and the sign at the gate. This post collects them anyway, in order, from a Saturday on the Santa Monica
    Pier in 2021 to Christmas Day at LEGOLAND in 2024.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pacific-park-2021">Pacific Park, Santa Monica Pier · October 9, 2021</h2>
  <p>
    The smallest park on this list, and the only one you can walk into for free. <strong>Pacific Park</strong> sits
    on the end of the Santa Monica Pier, where Route 66 runs out of land, and its octopus-shaped arch of steel loops
    leads to a solar-powered Ferris wheel and a little yellow roller coaster above the beach. I went on a sunny
    October Saturday with friends, and we bought tickets for the wheel. From the top, the West Coaster's yellow
    track ran along below us, with the beach and the whole curve of Santa Monica Bay beyond it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pp-arch.webp'} alt="The looping steel arch of Pacific Park against a bright sun, with game booths below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Pacific Park arch, 10:22 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pp-from-wheel.webp'} alt="Looking down from the Ferris wheel onto a yellow roller coaster track, the pier and a wide sandy beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the top of the wheel.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Afterwards, from the sand, the wheel and the coaster looked like a toy set on stilts above the beach.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pp-beach.webp'} alt="The Pacific Park Ferris wheel and yellow roller coaster on the pier, seen from the beach under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pier from the beach, 1:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="hhn-2021">Halloween Horror Nights, Universal Hollywood · October 14, 2021</h2>
  <p>
    Five days later came the biggest night of that autumn. A group of us, all grad students at USC, went to
    <strong>Halloween Horror Nights</strong> at Universal Studios Hollywood, the park's after-dark Halloween
    event of haunted mazes and roaming scare actors. The evening started at the studio store, wrapped in horror
    posters, and at the Universal globe, lit purple and fogged up for the occasion.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhn-store.webp'} alt="The Universal Studio Store at night, its facade covered in Halloween Horror Nights posters." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The studio store, 7:16 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhn-globe.webp'} alt="The Universal Studios globe lit purple at night, with fog around its base and a crowd nearby." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The globe, in Halloween purple.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Inside, the park was its normal self in the dark: Springfield from <em>The Simpsons</em> with Krustyland
    glowing in neon, and Hogwarts castle lit blue on its hill above the Wizarding World. Between them, signs pointed
    the way to that year's mazes, among them Netflix's <em>The Haunting of Hill House</em> and <em>The Curse of
    Pandora's Box</em>. We did some rides too, though I no longer remember which ones.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhn-springfield.webp'} alt="Springfield's Krustyland at night, bright with neon signs, with a crowd in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Krustyland, 7:46 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhn-hogwarts.webp'} alt="Hogwarts castle lit blue against the night sky above dark trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hogwarts, 8 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last photo of the night was taken at 1:22 in the morning, on the way out, under the neon Universal Studios sign.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhn-maze-sign.webp'} alt="A Halloween Horror Nights sign pointing to The Haunting of Hill House and The Curse of Pandora's Box." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Choose your maze.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhn-neon.webp'} alt="The neon Universal Studios Hollywood sign glowing red, green and blue at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The way out, 1:22 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Six Flags Magic Mountain · August 24, 2022</h2>
  <p>
    The thrill-ride park of Southern California, an hour north of Los Angeles in Valencia, with more roller coasters
    than almost any park in the world. My wife and I went on a summer Wednesday and rode many of them; which ones,
    I can no longer say. There is exactly one record of the day: a twelve-second video from the front of a log
    flume. Thrill rides are hard on photography, and that may be the point.
  </p>

  <h2>Universal Orlando and SeaWorld · August 11–12, 2024</h2>
  <p>
    On our 2024 road trip we gave Orlando two days. The first was <strong>Universal Studios Florida</strong>,
    where Optimus Prime guards the entrance to Transformers: The Ride and a whole Springfield has been built as a
    walk-through cartoon town, statue of Jebediah Springfield included. Lunch was at Krusty Burger: a burger on a
    bun branded with a K, curly fries and a drink in a souvenir cup.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-transformers.webp'} alt="A giant Optimus Prime figure above the entrance to Transformers: The Ride 3D at Universal Studios Florida." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Transformers, 11:32 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'orl-krusty-burger.webp'} alt="A Krusty Burger tray with a burger, curly fries and a drink in a souvenir cup." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch at Krusty Burger.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We also came home with a Simpsons magnet from Springfield. On our fridge it pretends to come from
    <a href="/misc/springfield-2024">the real Springfield</a>, the town in Oregon the cartoon is named after. It
    really came from Florida.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-jebediah.webp'} alt="A statue of Jebediah Springfield from The Simpsons on a stone plinth at Universal Studios." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Jebediah Springfield, founder.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'orl-lagoon.webp'} alt="The lagoon at Universal Studios Florida, with a long colorful building on the far shore under big clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Across the lagoon, 12:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The second day was <strong>SeaWorld Orlando</strong>. We went in, and the camera roll has nothing from inside
    the gates, which might be its own kind of review. The whole
    Orlando stretch is in the <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">road trip post</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disneyland-2024">Disneyland at Halloween · October 13, 2024</h2>
  <p>
    Back home, Disneyland was dressed for Halloween. We started at <strong>Disney California Adventure</strong>,
    where Pixar Pier lines the lagoon with the Incredicoaster and the Mickey wheel, and where the newest ride that
    year was <em>Inside Out Emotional Whirlwind</em>: Joy and Disgust standing over a track of glowing memory orbs.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dl-pixar-pier.webp'} alt="Pixar Pier across the lagoon at Disney California Adventure, with a roller coaster and a big Ferris wheel." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pixar Pier, 2:22 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dl-whirlwind.webp'} alt="The Inside Out Emotional Whirlwind ride sign with a figure of Joy, in front of a white coaster track." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Inside Out, 3:38 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then across the plaza into <strong>Disneyland Park</strong>, where the Partners statue of Walt and Mickey stood
    in front of Sleeping Beauty Castle surrounded by orange pumpkins. On Main Street a giant Mickey-shaped pumpkin
    sat outside the shops.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dl-mickey-pumpkin.webp'} alt="A giant jack-o'-lantern shaped like Mickey Mouse's head on Main Street, U.S.A., in front of a cream building with green shutters, hung with orange and yellow bunting, with a crowd around it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Main Street, 5:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    As the sun went down, a yellow submarine glided around the lagoon at the foot of the Matterhorn.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dl-castle.webp'} alt="The Partners statue of Walt Disney and Mickey in front of Sleeping Beauty Castle, with Halloween pumpkins around it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The castle, with pumpkins.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dl-matterhorn.webp'} alt="The Matterhorn at dusk, with a yellow submarine in the lagoon below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Matterhorn at dusk, 6:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>LEGOLAND on Christmas Day · December 25, 2024</h2>
  <p>
    And the gentlest park of all. On Christmas Day we drove down to <strong>LEGOLAND California</strong> in
    Carlsbad, where the Christmas tree is made of bricks and Miniland rebuilds whole cities at about a twentieth of
    their size, from Washington to a little London on an island. That day is told in full in the
    <a href="/misc/san-diego">San Diego post</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'legoland-tree.webp'} alt="A tall Christmas tree built from green LEGO bricks with brick ornaments and a gold star." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Every needle a brick.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'miniland-london.webp'} alt="LEGO models of Big Ben and the London Eye on a small island in a pond, framed by trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>London in Miniland.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Off the rides</h2>
  <p>
    Looking at all of these together, I notice that I remember the rides and photographed everything else. I can't
    name a single maze from Horror Nights or a single coaster from Magic Mountain, but I can tell you what the
    burger at Krusty Burger looked like. Maybe that is the right way round. The rides were for being there; the
    photos are for what was around them.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Built from the photos' timestamps and locations, and from our own notes; times are local.
    Photo locations have been removed from the published images.</small></p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Some photos in this post are my wife's.</small></p>
  <Comments />
</BaseLayout>

